-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/81058854-1746-42e7-bc69-5bacf1bb3e18
-- title:
--   $\mathcal S$ is an abelian subgroup of $\mathcal T$ commuting with all of $\mathcal T$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge1$ and let $\mathcal S$ be the group generated by $S^X_i = \prod_j X_{i,j}X_{i+1,j}$ and $S^Z_j = \prod_i Z_{i,j}Z_{i,j+1}$, $i,j = 1,\dots,n-1$ (modulo phase). Then:
--
--   1. $\mathcal S \subseteq \mathcal T$;
--   2. any two elements of $\mathcal S$ commute;
--   3. every element of $\mathcal S$ commutes with every element of $\mathcal T$;
--   4. $\mathcal S$ consists exactly of the $P(a,b)$ whose $X$-part consists of an even number of full rows and whose $Z$-part consists of an even number of full columns:
--
--   $$
--   (a,b)\in\mathcal S \iff a_{i,j} = a_{i,j'}\ \forall i,j,j',\ \ \textstyle\sum_i a_{i,1} = 0,\ \ b_{i,j} = b_{i',j}\ \forall i,i',j,\ \ \sum_j b_{1,j} = 0 .
--   $$
--
--   Clause 4 shows that the generated group (11) is the set the paper describes in words before (10).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: lattice size n, rows and columns indexed 1..n, generators indexed by Z_{n−1} → n = m + 1 with m : ℕ; rows and columns Fin (m + 1), 0-based; generators indexed by Fin m acting on i.castSucc, i.succ; reason: Lean convention; n = 0 has no qubits, so every lattice of the paper is an instance
--   - encoding: Pauli operators with phases ±1, ±i (the (−1)^φ of (8), (13)) → pairs (a, b) of bit matrices modulo phase; multiplication is addition, commutation is the symplectic bit symp; reason: membership in T, S, C(S), commutation and weight do not depend on the phase
--   - reading: S as the set (10): ⊕_i(∧_j a_ij) = 0, ⊕_j(∧_i b_ij) = 0 → S = the group generated by S^X_i, S^Z_j of (11) (SGroup m); reason: (10) taken alone admits non-full rows (n = 2: a single X_{1,1} satisfies it but anticommutes with Z_{1,1}Z_{1,2} ∈ T, contrary to p.4); the prose before (10) and the generators (11) agree, and S_props proves that the span equals the prose set
-- source:
--   D. Bacon, Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories, arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, p.4, after (10), and eqs. (10)–(11)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_InT
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_symp
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_SGroup

namespace BaconOQEC

/-- Bacon, *Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories*,
arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, p.4, after (10), and (11): "S is also a group. In fact it is an
Abelian subgroup of T. Further all of the elements of S commute not just with each other, but with
all of the elements of T. It can be generated by nearest row and column operators", with `S`
described before (10) as the Pauli operators "made up of an even number of rows consisting
entirely of X operators and an even number of columns consisting entirely of Z operators". For
the group `S` generated by the `S^X_i`, `S^Z_j` of (11)–(12):
1. every element of `S` lies in `T`;
2. any two elements of `S` commute;
3. every element of `S` commutes with every element of `T`;
4. `S` is exactly the set of `P(a, b)` with every row of `a` constant (all `X` or no `X`) and an
   even number of full `X`-rows, and every column of `b` constant with an even number of full
   `Z`-columns.

**Formalization Note.** Indices are 0-based (`Fin (m + 1)`, lattice size `n = m + 1`, so `n ≥ 1`); Pauli operators
modulo phase are pairs `(a, b)` of bit matrices; commutation is `symp = 0`. Clause 4 makes the reading of the
printed (10) explicit: (10)'s condition `⊕_i(∧_j a_{i,j}) = 0` taken alone would admit operators
whose rows are not full. "Even number of full rows" is written as `Σ_i a_{i,0} = 0` in `ZMod 2`,
given that each row is constant. -/
theorem S_props (m : ℕ) :
    (∀ v ∈ SGroup m, InT v.1 v.2) ∧
    (∀ v ∈ SGroup m, ∀ w ∈ SGroup m, symp v w = 0) ∧
    (∀ v ∈ SGroup m, ∀ w : Bits m × Bits m, InT w.1 w.2 → symp v w = 0) ∧
    (∀ v : Bits m × Bits m, v ∈ SGroup m ↔
      ((∀ i j j', v.1 i j = v.1 i j') ∧ (∑ i, v.1 i 0 = 0) ∧
        (∀ i i' j, v.2 i j = v.2 i' j) ∧ (∑ j, v.2 0 j = 0))) := by sorry

end BaconOQEC
